[?25l[0m[38;5;141m> [0m[38;5;252m[1m## What was wrong and what I changed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mProblem:[22m The Rust translation was missing a C-ABI export for the [38;5;10mSPX_gen_message_random[0m function. The internal Rust function [38;5;10mhash::gen_message_random[0m existed and was correctly implemented, but the test framework (cando2) dynamically loads [38;5;10mlibsha2.so[0m and looks up the symbol [38;5;10mSPX_gen_message_random[0m via [38;5;10mdlsym[0m. Since no [38;5;10m#[no_mangle] extern "C"[0m function with that name was exported, the test runner panicked with [38;5;10mundefined symbol: SPX_gen_message_random[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mFix:[22m Added a single FFI wrapper function in [38;5;10mlib.rs[0m:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mrust
[0m[38;5;10m#[unsafe(no_mangle)]
pub unsafe extern "C" fn SPX_gen_message_random(
    r: *mut c_uchar, sk_prf: *const c_uchar, optrand: *const c_uchar,
    m: *const c_uchar, mlen: c_ulonglong, ctx: *const context::SpxCtx,
) {
    // convert raw pointers to slices, then delegate to the Rust implementation
    hash::gen_message_random(r, sk_prf, optrand, m, mlen, ctx);
}
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This follows the same pattern already used in [38;5;10mlib.rs[0m for other exported functions like [38;5;10mcrypto_sign[0m, [38;5;10mcrypto_sign_keypair[0m, etc. — the translation had simply omitted the export for this particular function. The underlying HMAC-SHA256 implementation in [38;5;10mhash.rs[0m was correct; it just wasn't reachable from the C ABI.[0m[0m
